{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>I shall see him, but not now</b> etc. He doubled the matters in different words — he doubled to say <b>There shall step a star</b> etc. <b>and a scepter shall rise</b> etc., and he doubled to say <b>and shall smite</b> etc. <b>and destroy</b> etc. The entire prophecy is said concerning King Messiah, and it will be explained according to the words of the Sages, may their memory be a blessing (Sanhedrin 98a), who said concerning the verse ״בְּעִתָּהּ אֲחִישֶׁנָּה״ — if they merit, אֲחִישֶׁנָּה (hastened); if they do not merit, בְּעִתָּהּ (in its time), and that is far, exceedingly distant. And this is your sign: we are now at the beginning of the sixth hundred-year century of the sixth millennium, and corresponding to these two reckonings the verse spoke. Corresponding to if they merit, he said <b>I shall see him</b> — meaning, a thing that I am about to say, but it is not now, at this time, but at another time; and it may even be that it is not so very far, for they said, may their memory be a blessing (Deuteronomy Rabbah 2:23), that if Israel would return in repentance as required they would be redeemed immediately, even if but a single day had passed over them in exile, as it is written ״יַעַנְךָ ה׳ בְּיוֹם צָרָה״. And corresponding to the reckoning of ״בְּעִתָּהּ״ he said <b>I shall behold him</b> — as one gazing from afar, and he said <b>but not near</b>, for the reckoning of בְּעִתָּהּ is very long on account of our sins. And his saying <b>There shall step</b> etc. means: this is the prophecy about which he said ״I shall see him״ etc." "<b>And the doubling</b> of the matter and the change of expression will be explained according to their words, may their memory be a blessing (Sanhedrin 98a), who said that if the redemption comes through the merit of Israel, the matter will be wondrously exalted and the redeemer of Israel will be revealed from heaven with a sign and wonder, as stated in the Zohar (vol. 1, 119a, and vol. 3, 272b) — whereas when the redemption comes by way of the appointed time and Israel are not worthy of it, it will be in a different manner, and concerning this it is said (Zechariah 9:9) that the redeemer will come ״עָנִי וְרוֹכֵב עַל חֲמוֹר״. And this is what he said here corresponding to the redemption of ״אֲחִישֶׁנָּה,״ which is through the merit of Israel, which he hinted at in the statement ״I shall see him, but not now״ — he said <b>There shall step a star</b>, that the redeemer shall shine from heaven; he also hinted at the star that will appear in the midst of the sky as a wondrous sign, as stated in the Zohar (ibid.). And corresponding to the redemption of ״בְּעִתָּהּ,״ which he hinted at in the statement ״I shall behold him, but not near,״ he said <b>and a scepter shall rise out of Israel</b> — meaning, one tribe of Israel will arise in the manner of those who rise in the world through nature, as it says (Daniel 4:14) ״וּשְׁפַל אֲנָשִׁים יְקִים עֲלַהּ״, that he will come poor and riding on a donkey and will arise and rule and do what is stated in the following verses." "<b>And the reason</b> that he designated the redemption of ״אֲחִישֶׁנָּה״ — which is like a star — to Jacob, and the redemption of ״בְּעִתָּהּ״ — which is poor etc. — to Israel, who are the class of the righteous, is intended to say that the apprehension of this mode of the redeemer's coming as a star is when the redemption will come from the aspect of the rank of the intermediate among Israel, that they will improve their deeds and he will come through their merit — and that is his saying <b>There shall step a star from Jacob</b>; but his coming by way of ״and a scepter shall rise״ will be when the class of the intermediate do not improve their deeds, and he will come only from the aspect of the class of the righteous among them — and that is his saying <b>and a scepter shall rise out of Israel</b>." "<b>Further,</b> he hinted in the statement <b>There shall step a star from Jacob</b> at Messiah son of David, who is hinted at in the star, as mentioned; and his saying <b>and a scepter shall rise out of Israel</b> hints at Messiah son of Ephraim. The intention in this is that if Israel are in a state where they are called Jacob, only Messiah son of David will shine for them; but Messiah son of Ephraim will die in the first war, when Armilus will kill him, as they said, may their memory be a blessing (Sukkah 52a). But if Israel are all righteous, so that they are designated by the name Israel, then even that tribe which is the Messiah who comes from Ephraim — <b>shall rise</b>, meaning, he will have standing before his enemies and Armilus will not kill him. And you will find that the great ones of Israel commanded (Etz Chaim, Sha'ar HaAmidah) to intend in our prayer, when we say ״לִישׁוּעָתְךָ קִוִּינוּ״ etc., to seek mercy for Messiah son of Ephraim, that he not be killed in the war." "And his saying <b>and shall smite</b> etc. will be explained according to their words (Genesis Rabbah 44:23), who said that three nations of which HaShem told Abraham, apart from the seven whose time had already come within reach of Israel's hand, are Ammon and Moab and Edom, and they are hinted at in the expression Kenite, Kenizzite, Kadmonite. And that is his saying <b>and shall smite the corners of Moab</b> — meaning, the Messiah spoken of will destroy Moab and leave it no corner in any of its angles; and for this reason he was precise to say ״corners.״" "<b>Or</b> it may allude that David will come first and smite them and trample them, and King Mashiach will destroy the remnants of their edges. And concerning Ammon he said <b>and he shall tear down all the children of Shet</b>, and he attributed them to Shet because they are bare of Shet (i.e., exposed to shame), and because their name contains the quality of shame (boshet), therefore he said ״וְקַרְקַר״ and did not say ״וּמָחַץ״:" "<b>Or</b> the verse may intend the totality of all the nations, for all of them descended from Shet, and Ammon is included among them. And the reason he specified Moav is because they sent for him and it is with them that he is speaking; therefore he explained the matters that pertain to them in particular." "And if you should say: but Israel has only ten nations, so why did he say ״כָּל בְּנֵי שֵׁת״? That is what the verse concludes: <b>and Edom shall be a possession</b> etc., and from now on all the children of Shet who come to acquire merit will be purified for us through Edom, and therefore <b>and he shall tear down all the children of Shet</b> and he did not say ״וּמָחַץ״ — for not all the nations will be destroyed in the time to come, and concerning them it is said in the prophecy (Isaiah 65:20) ״the youth a hundred years old״ etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {3 "The name רוֹמִילוּס is rendered as Armilus, the eschatological enemy figure, following the context of Sukkah 52a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:24.230000243Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}